CertHub.register({"id":"aws-saa","vendor":"Amazon Web Services","name":"AWS Certified Solutions Architect – Associate","short":"Solutions Architect Associate","exam":"SAA-C03","blurb":"For people who design systems on AWS: choosing and combining services into architectures that are secure, resilient, high-performing and cost-optimized, from IAM and VPC design to storage, databases, decoupling, disaster recovery and pricing models.","status":"check","statusNote":"Domains and weights (Design Secure Architectures 30%, Design Resilient Architectures 26%, Design High-Performing Architectures 24%, Design Cost-Optimized Architectures 20%) are taken from the published SAA-C03 exam guide as best known; the AWS certification pages could not be opened from this environment on Sept 25, 2026. Confirm the domains, weights, exam length and passing score on the official exam page and exam guide before booking, and check there whether AWS has announced a replacement exam version.","lastVerified":"2026-09-25","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"65 (50 scored, 15 unscored; multiple choice and multiple response)","minutes":130,"pass":"720 on a 100–1000 scale","extra":"AWS recommends at least one year of hands-on experience designing cloud solutions on AWS. The certification is valid for three years."},"examSim":{"questions":65,"minutes":130},"sources":[{"label":"AWS Certified Solutions Architect – Associate exam page","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/certification/certified-solutions-architect-associate/"},{"label":"SAA-C03 exam guide (AWS documentation)","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/solutions-architect-associate-03/solutions-architect-associate-03.html"}],"planWeeks":12,"hoursPerWeek":"6–8","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Design Secure Architectures","w":30,"topics":["IAM users, groups, roles and policies: least privilege, identity-based vs resource-based policies and policy evaluation logic","Multi-account security: AWS Organizations, service control policies, IAM Identity Center and cross-account roles","Federation and temporary credentials: STS AssumeRole, SAML and OIDC federation, Cognito user pools vs identity pools","VPC security layers: security groups vs network ACLs, public and private subnets, NAT gateways, bastion hosts vs Session Manager","Private access to AWS services: gateway endpoints, interface endpoints (PrivateLink) and endpoint policies","Protecting the edge: AWS WAF, Shield Standard vs Shield Advanced, and CloudFront with origin access control","Encryption at rest with AWS KMS: AWS managed vs customer managed keys, key policies, envelope encryption and S3 SSE-S3, SSE-KMS and SSE-C","Encryption in transit: ACM certificates, TLS on ALB and CloudFront, and enforcing HTTPS with aws:SecureTransport","Secrets management: Secrets Manager rotation vs Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString","S3 data protection: Block Public Access, bucket policies, presigned URLs, versioning, MFA Delete and Object Lock modes","Detection and compliance services: CloudTrail, AWS Config rules, GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie and Security Hub"],"notes":["Exam guide task statements 1.1 Design secure access to AWS resources; 1.2 Design secure workloads and applications; 1.3 Determine appropriate data security controls"],"labs":["Free Tier: create an IAM role that EC2 can assume with read-only access to one S3 bucket, launch a t2.micro or t3.micro instance with that instance profile, and run `aws s3 ls s3://your-bucket` from it with no access keys configured. Then try to list a different bucket and read the AccessDenied error. Terminate the instance and delete the bucket afterwards.","Free Tier: build a VPC with one public and one private subnet (no NAT gateway, which bills by the hour), put a security group that only allows HTTP from your IP on a public instance, then add a network ACL deny rule for your IP and watch it override the security group. Connect with Session Manager instead of SSH. Terminate the instance and delete the VPC when done.","Free Tier: create a customer managed KMS key, upload an object to S3 with SSE-KMS using it, and find the matching Decrypt event in CloudTrail event history when you download it. Add a bucket policy that denies requests where aws:SecureTransport is false. Cost warning: each customer managed key costs about $1 a month, so schedule it for deletion (7-day minimum) when you finish."]},{"id":2,"name":"Design Resilient Architectures","w":26,"topics":["Multi-AZ web tiers: Elastic Load Balancing (ALB vs NLB), Auto Scaling groups and ELB health checks","Decoupling with Amazon SQS (standard vs FIFO, visibility timeout, dead-letter queues) and SNS fan-out","Event-driven and serverless patterns: EventBridge, Lambda, Step Functions and API Gateway","Containers on AWS: ECS vs EKS, and the Fargate vs EC2 launch types","Relational database resilience: RDS Multi-AZ, read replicas, Aurora replicas and Aurora Global Database","DynamoDB resilience: global tables, point-in-time recovery and on-demand backups","Route 53 routing policies and health checks: failover, weighted, latency, geolocation and multivalue","Disaster recovery strategies: backup and restore, pilot light, warm standby and multi-site active-active, matched to RPO and RTO","Backup and replication: AWS Backup plans, S3 Cross-Region Replication, EBS snapshot and AMI copies, AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","Resilient hybrid networking: Site-to-Site VPN, Direct Connect with VPN backup, and Transit Gateway","Infrastructure as code and service quotas: CloudFormation, StackSets and planning for limits and throttling"],"notes":["Exam guide task statements 2.1 Design scalable and loosely coupled architectures; 2.2 Design highly available and/or fault-tolerant architectures"],"labs":["Free Tier: create a launch template for a t2.micro or t3.micro that serves a simple page with user data, put it in an Auto Scaling group across two AZs (min 2) behind an ALB with ELB health checks, then terminate one instance and watch the group replace it. Cost warning: an ALB bills by the hour and may not be covered by your Free Tier plan, so delete the ALB and the Auto Scaling group within the hour.","Free Tier: build an SNS topic that fans out to two SQS queues, one of which has a dead-letter queue with maxReceiveCount 2. Publish messages, receive one without deleting it until it lands in the DLQ, and read the queue metrics in CloudWatch. SQS and SNS stay free at this scale; delete the topic and queues afterwards.","Free Tier: create a DynamoDB table in on-demand mode, turn on point-in-time recovery, add and then change some items, and restore the table to a time before the change as a new table. Also create an AWS Backup on-demand job for it. Cost warning: PITR and backup storage bill per GB after any Free Tier allowance, so delete both tables and the backup when done."]},{"id":3,"name":"Design High-Performing Architectures","w":24,"topics":["EC2 instance families and placement groups (cluster, spread, partition), and enhanced networking with ENA and EFA","EBS volume types and instance store: gp3 vs io2 Block Express vs st1 and sc1","Shared file systems: Amazon EFS vs FSx for Windows File Server, FSx for Lustre and FSx for NetApp ONTAP","S3 performance: prefixes, multipart upload, byte-range fetches and S3 Transfer Acceleration","Caching: ElastiCache for Redis vs Memcached, DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX), lazy loading vs write-through","Content delivery and global networking: CloudFront caching and TTLs vs AWS Global Accelerator","Choosing a database: RDS, Aurora, DynamoDB, Redshift, DocumentDB, Neptune, and RDS Proxy for connection pooling","DynamoDB performance: partition key design, provisioned vs on-demand capacity, auto scaling and secondary indexes","Streaming ingestion: Kinesis Data Streams vs Amazon Data Firehose vs Amazon MSK","Analytics services: Athena, AWS Glue, Lake Formation, EMR, Redshift Spectrum and QuickSight","EC2 Auto Scaling policies: target tracking, step, simple, scheduled and predictive scaling","Data migration and hybrid storage: DataSync, Snow Family, Storage Gateway, Transfer Family, DMS and SCT"],"notes":["Exam guide task statements 3.1 Determine high-performing and/or scalable storage solutions; 3.2 Design high-performing and elastic compute solutions; 3.3 Determine high-performing database solutions; 3.4 Determine high-performing and/or scalable network architectures; 3.5 Determine high-performing data ingestion and transformation solutions"],"labs":["Free Tier: create an S3 bucket in a Region far from you, upload a 200 MB file once with a normal `aws s3 cp` and once with the accelerate endpoint turned on (`aws configure set default.s3.use_accelerate_endpoint true`), and compare times; the CLI uses multipart upload automatically for large files. Cost warning: Transfer Acceleration is charged per GB, so use a single small test file, then delete the bucket.","Free Tier: upload a few CSV or JSON log files to S3, create a table in the Glue Data Catalog with an Athena CREATE TABLE statement, and run queries that filter and count rows; note the data scanned per query. Athena bills per TB scanned, so keep the data to a few MB and delete the bucket and query results afterwards.","Free Tier: in an Auto Scaling group of t2.micro or t3.micro instances (min 1, max 3), add a target tracking policy for 50% average CPU, run a CPU stress command on the instance, and watch the alarms and scaling activity history. Then add a scheduled action. Delete the group and launch template when done so no instances keep running."]},{"id":4,"name":"Design Cost-Optimized Architectures","w":20,"topics":["EC2 purchase options: On-Demand, Reserved Instances, Compute vs EC2 Instance Savings Plans, Spot, Dedicated Instances and Dedicated Hosts","Spot Instances in practice: interruption notices, mixed-instances Auto Scaling groups and allocation strategies","Right-sizing compute: AWS Compute Optimizer, Graviton instances, and serverless vs always-on cost models","S3 storage classes: Standard, Intelligent-Tiering, Standard-IA, One Zone-IA and the three Glacier classes","S3 Lifecycle rules, S3 Storage Lens and Requester Pays","Cutting EBS and backup costs: gp2 to gp3, Data Lifecycle Manager, snapshot archive and unattached volumes","Database cost choices: DynamoDB on-demand vs provisioned, Aurora Serverless v2, reserved DB instances and stopping idle databases","Data transfer costs: inter-AZ, inter-Region and internet egress, NAT gateway charges vs gateway endpoints, and CloudFront","Cost visibility tools: Cost Explorer, AWS Budgets, Cost and Usage Reports, cost allocation tags and Trusted Advisor","Consolidated billing in AWS Organizations: volume discounts and sharing Reserved Instance and Savings Plans benefits"],"notes":["Exam guide task statements 4.1 Design cost-optimized storage solutions; 4.2 Design cost-optimized compute solutions; 4.3 Design cost-optimized database solutions; 4.4 Design cost-optimized network architectures"],"labs":["Free Tier: in the Billing console create a monthly cost budget of $5 with email alerts at 50% of actual spend and 100% of forecasted spend, activate a cost allocation tag such as Project, and open Cost Explorer grouped by service. Budgets and Cost Explorer's console views are free; do this first before any other lab.","Free Tier: create an S3 bucket with a lifecycle rule that moves objects to Standard-IA after 30 days, to Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days and expires them after 365 days, and another rule that aborts incomplete multipart uploads after 7 days. Upload a few test files and review the rule in the console. Delete the bucket when done.","Free Tier: open the EC2 console's Spot placement score and Spot pricing history for two instance types, then use the AWS Pricing Calculator to compare On-Demand, a one-year Compute Savings Plan and Spot for a small three-instance web tier. No resources are launched, so this costs nothing."]}],"study":{"1":[["How does IAM decide whether a request is allowed?","Everything starts as an implicit deny. An explicit deny in any applicable policy (SCP, permissions boundary, session policy, identity or resource policy) wins. Otherwise the request needs an allow from an identity or resource policy, and the SCPs and any boundary must also allow it."],["When should you use an IAM role instead of an IAM user?","Whenever a workload or person can get temporary credentials: EC2 instance profiles, Lambda execution roles, cross-account access, federation and IAM Identity Center. IAM users with long-term access keys are for rare cases that cannot use roles."],["Compare security groups and network ACLs.","Security groups attach to network interfaces, are stateful and only allow traffic; rules are all evaluated together. Network ACLs attach to subnets, are stateless so return traffic needs rules, and evaluate numbered allow and deny rules in order."],["What are the three S3 server-side encryption options, and when is each chosen?","SSE-S3 uses keys S3 manages and is the default. SSE-KMS uses a KMS key, which adds key policies, CloudTrail logging and the option to disable the key. SSE-C uses a key the client supplies on every request, which AWS does not store."],["Gateway endpoint or interface endpoint?","Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB, work through route tables and have no charge. Interface endpoints (PrivateLink) are ENIs with private IPs in your subnets, work for most AWS services and partner services, and bill hourly plus per GB."],["Which detection service answers which question?","CloudTrail: who called which API. Config: what a resource looked like and whether it complies with rules. GuardDuty: is something malicious happening. Inspector: which workloads have vulnerabilities. Macie: where sensitive data lives in S3. Security Hub: one view of findings and standards checks."]],"2":[["What makes a web tier highly available on AWS?","Instances in at least two AZs, a load balancer that health-checks them, and an Auto Scaling group using ELB health checks to replace failed instances. Keep instances stateless by storing sessions in ElastiCache or DynamoDB and files in S3 or EFS."],["Compare SQS standard and FIFO queues.","Standard queues give nearly unlimited throughput, at-least-once delivery and best-effort ordering. FIFO queues give ordering within each message group and exactly-once processing via deduplication, with lower throughput limits."],["Contrast RDS Multi-AZ and read replicas.","Multi-AZ is for availability: synchronous replication to a standby in another AZ with automatic failover on the same endpoint. Read replicas are for read scaling: asynchronous copies, in-Region or cross-Region, that serve reads and can be promoted manually."],["List the four DR strategies from cheapest to fastest.","Backup and restore (hours, lowest cost), pilot light (core data live, rest launched on demand, tens of minutes), warm standby (scaled-down full copy running, minutes), and multi-site active-active (near zero downtime, highest cost)."],["What is the difference between RPO and RTO?","RPO is how much data loss is acceptable, measured back from the failure (drives backup and replication frequency). RTO is how long the service can be down before it is restored (drives how much must already be running)."],["When would you choose Route 53 failover, weighted, latency or geolocation routing?","Failover for active-passive DR with health checks; weighted for splitting traffic such as canary releases; latency to send users to the Region with the lowest latency for them; geolocation to route by the user's country or continent, for example for content rules."]],"3":[["Compare the three placement group types.","Cluster packs instances close together in one AZ for low latency and high throughput. Spread puts each instance on separate hardware (up to seven per AZ per group) for critical instances. Partition groups instances into partitions on separate racks, for large distributed systems such as Hadoop or Cassandra."],["How do you choose between EFS, FSx for Windows, FSx for Lustre and FSx for NetApp ONTAP?","EFS for shared NFS storage for Linux. FSx for Windows for SMB shares with Active Directory and NTFS. FSx for Lustre for HPC and ML throughput, often linked to S3. FSx for NetApp ONTAP for multi-protocol (NFS, SMB, iSCSI) and NetApp features such as SnapMirror."],["Compare lazy loading and write-through caching.","Lazy loading fills the cache only on a miss, so it caches only what is read but can serve stale data until the TTL expires. Write-through updates the cache on every write, so data is fresh but the cache holds items that may never be read. Many designs combine both with a TTL."],["CloudFront or Global Accelerator?","CloudFront caches HTTP and HTTPS content at edge locations and can accelerate dynamic content. Global Accelerator gives two static anycast IPs, routes TCP and UDP traffic over the AWS network to regional endpoints, and fails over quickly without DNS caching delays; it does not cache."],["Kinesis Data Streams, Data Firehose or SQS?","Data Streams for real-time streams with several consumers and replay within the retention period. Firehose for managed delivery to S3, Redshift, OpenSearch or HTTP endpoints with buffering and format conversion. SQS for decoupled work items that one consumer processes and then deletes."],["How do you migrate a large data set when the network is slow?","Work out the transfer time first: 10 TB over 100 Mbps takes about 9 days at full use. If it is too slow, use a Snowball Edge device offline; for ongoing or online transfers use DataSync, and for databases use DMS (with SCT when the engine changes)."]],"4":[["Compare Reserved Instances and Savings Plans.","Both give up to about 72% off On-Demand for a one- or three-year commitment. Standard RIs are tied to an instance family, Region and OS (Convertible RIs can be exchanged). Compute Savings Plans commit to $/hour and apply across families, Regions, Fargate and Lambda; EC2 Instance Savings Plans are tied to a family in a Region for a bigger discount."],["When are Spot Instances a good fit, and how do you make them safe?","For stateless, fault-tolerant or flexible work such as batch, CI, rendering and big data. Handle the two-minute interruption notice, checkpoint work, diversify across instance types and AZs, and use the price-capacity-optimized allocation strategy in Auto Scaling groups or EC2 Fleet."],["Walk through the S3 storage classes from most to least expensive to store.","Standard; Intelligent-Tiering (automatic, small monitoring fee); Standard-IA and One Zone-IA (retrieval fees, 30-day minimum); Glacier Instant Retrieval (milliseconds, quarterly access); Glacier Flexible Retrieval (minutes to hours); Glacier Deep Archive (12 to 48 hours, cheapest)."],["Which data transfer charges matter most in designs?","Inbound from the internet is free. Outbound to the internet, between Regions and between AZs is charged per GB. NAT gateways add per-GB processing charges, so gateway endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB and CloudFront for internet delivery are common savings."],["What tools help find and control AWS costs?","Cost Explorer for analysis and forecasts, AWS Budgets for alerts and actions, the Cost and Usage Report for detailed line items, cost allocation tags for grouping, Compute Optimizer and Trusted Advisor for right-sizing and idle resources, and consolidated billing for shared discounts."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true,"hasPbqs":true});
